      correctIndex: 0,
      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Stock Dividends: Journal Entry Analysis</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">Debit Retained Earnings $250,000; Credit Common Stock Dividend Distributable $10,000 and Additional Paid-In Capital $240,000</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Explanation of Stock Dividend Accounting</h3><p style="color:#01506e; font-weight:600; margin:14px 0 6px 0">Understanding Small vs. Large Stock Dividends</p><p style="margin:0">When accounting for stock dividends, the classification as &quot;small&quot; or &quot;large&quot; determines the accounting treatment:</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Small Stock Dividend: Less than 20-25% of outstanding shares</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Large Stock Dividend: Greater than 20-25% of outstanding shares</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Analysis of the Current Situation</h3><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Outstanding shares: 200,000 shares of $1 par value common stock</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Stock dividend percentage: 5%</li></ul><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Number of new shares: 200,000 × 5% = 10,000 shares</strong></div><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Market price per share: $25</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Par value per share: $1</li></ul><p style="margin:0">Since this is a 5% dividend (well below the 20-25% threshold), it is considered a small stock dividend.</p></div><div style="background-color:#f5f9fc; border-left:5px solid #207bb5; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Accounting Treatment for Small Stock Dividends</h3><p style="margin:0">For small stock dividends, US GAAP requires that the dividend be recorded at fair market value rather than par value.</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The total fair market value is calculated as: Number of shares × Market price</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">This amount is debited from Retained Earnings</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The par value of new shares is credited to Common Stock Dividend Distributable</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The difference between fair value and par value is credited to Additional Paid-In Capital</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#eef9f2; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Calculation Details</h3><table class="mx-table" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Component</th><th class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Calculation</th><th class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Fair Market Value</td><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">10,000 shares × $25 per share</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$250,000</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Par Value</td><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">10,000 shares × $1 par value</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$10,000</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Excess over Par Value</td><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$250,000 - $10,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$240,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">Small stock dividends (less than 20-25% of outstanding shares) like the 5% dividend in this example are recorded at market value. The journal entry debits Retained Earnings for the full market value of the shares distributed ($250,000), credits Common Stock Dividend Distributable for the par value of the shares ($10,000), and credits Additional Paid-In Capital for the excess over par value ($240,000).</p><p style="margin:0">This accounting treatment reflects the economic reality that a small stock dividend effectively transfers value from retained earnings to permanent equity accounts in amounts based on the market value of the shares distributed.</p></div></div></div>`,
