      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Treasury Stock: Excess of Selling Price Over Cost</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">Credited to additional paid-in capital.</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Key Concept</h3><p style="margin:0">When treasury stock is sold at a price higher than its cost, the excess is not recognized as a gain in the income statement but is instead credited to additional paid-in capital.</p></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Accounting Explanation</h3><p style="margin:0">Treasury stock transactions are capital transactions, not income-generating activities. When treasury stock is sold above cost:</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The excess is recorded as additional paid-in capital from treasury stock transactions</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">No gain is recognized on the income statement</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">This approach maintains the distinction between operating activities and capital structure changes</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#f5f9fc; border-left:5px solid #207bb5; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Important Note</h3><p style="margin:0">If treasury stock is sold at a price lower than its cost, the deficit is debited to Additional Paid-in Capital from Treasury Stock. If no such account exists or if the balance is insufficient, the remaining deficit is charged to Retained Earnings.</p></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">Treasury stock transactions affect the capital structure of a company, not its earnings. The excess of selling price over cost for treasury stock is treated as additional paid-in capital, maintaining the distinction between operational and capital transactions.</p></div></div></div>`,
