      correctIndex: 2,
      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Stock Dividends and Shareholders&#x27; Equity</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">Remains unchanged as the value is merely transferred within equity.</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Explanation</h3><p style="margin:0">A stock dividend represents a distribution of additional shares to existing shareholders based on their current ownership percentage. Unlike cash dividends, stock dividends don&#x27;t reduce the company&#x27;s assets or total shareholders&#x27; equity.</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">When a company issues a stock dividend</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The number of outstanding shares increases</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The market price per share typically decreases proportionally</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The total market value of all shares remains approximately the same</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Total shareholders&#x27; equity remains unchanged</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Accounting Treatment</h3><p style="margin:0">A stock dividend requires a reclassification within the shareholders&#x27; equity section of the balance sheet. The value is transferred from Retained Earnings to the appropriate capital accounts (Common Stock and Additional Paid-in Capital).</p><p style="margin:0">Journal Entry for a 10% Stock Dividend</p><p style="margin:0">Analysis: Balance Sheet Impact</p><p style="margin:0">Let&#x27;s examine how the shareholders&#x27; equity section changes after a stock dividend:</p><table class="mx-table" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Equity Component</th><th class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Before Stock Dividend</th><th class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">After Stock Dividend</th><th class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Common Stock</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$50,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$55,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">+$5,000</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Additional Paid-in Capital</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$100,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$110,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">+$10,000</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Retained Earnings</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$200,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$185,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">-$15,000</td></tr><tr><td class="mx-txt" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Total Shareholders&#x27; Equity</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$350,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$350,000</td><td class="mx-num" style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">A stock dividend does not change the total shareholders&#x27; equity. It simply reallocates amounts within the equity section, transferring value from Retained Earnings to Common Stock and Additional Paid-in Capital accounts. The number of outstanding shares increases, but the total value of the company and shareholders&#x27; equity remains unchanged.</p></div></div></div>`,
